    "chunk_text": "3. Discipline exceptional powers with time and process: Finally, exceptional powers must be disciplined by time and process, not left to operate indefinitely. Many special regimes begin as temporary measures but evolve into permanent uncertainty. Restrictions should expire unless explicitly renewed, delays should trigger consequences rather than silence, and prolonged interventions should activate predictable compensation. Procedural tools such as time-bound decisions, deemed approvals, and parallel processing are not technical fixes; they are governance safeguards. Without them, delay remains the safest option and reform remains vulnerable to quiet resistance.\n\n(The author is a Policy and Advocacy Analyst at Advocata Institute)"
