    "md5": "006e5e27d8e0513e6f7e06af21d51217",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "India: Market cap exceeds GDP 139% , reflecting strong investor appetite and high valuations.\n\nHong Kong: Unique case\u2014its stock market is a global listing hub, so capitalisation dwarfs domestic GDP to 1400%.\n\nJapan: Mature, deep market with capitalisation nearly double GDP to 180%.\n\nSri Lanka: Lags far behind, with market cap only a fraction of GDP 20-25%, discouraging large foreign inflows.\n\nValuation advantage: P/E ratios around 10 make Sri Lanka cheap compared to India (~25).\n\nStructural weakness: Lack of depth, few investable companies, and dominance of SOEs outside the listed market.\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange does not adequately reflect the true structure of the national economy. Many of the country\u2019s most important sectors remain absent from the capital market \u2014 including major state-owned banks, utilities, insurance institutions, transport enterprises, and infrastructure companies.\n\nAs a result, the market remains narrow and lacks the scale required to attract significant foreign participation.\n\nIn particular, partial listings of state-owned enterprises (SOEs) would dramatically expand the capital market. This does not require full privatisation. The Government could retain majority ownership while offering 10\u201320 percent of shares to the public.\n\nFirst, it would increase market capitalisation, making Sri Lanka more visible and investable to global funds.\n\nSecond, it would improve liquidity, enabling large investors to participate without destabilising prices.\n\nThird, it would introduce stronger transparency and governance standards, as listed companies must comply with disclosure and regulatory requirements.\n\nFinally, it would allow citizens and employees to participate in the ownership of major national enterprises.\n\nIn successful economies, the stock market mirrors the real economy. Key industries are represented and investors can participate in their growth. Sri Lanka must move toward this model if it wishes to develop a deeper capital market."
